      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/creator": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "Paolo Ciccarese (https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5156-2703)"
      },
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "Silvio Peroni (http://orcid.org/0000-0003-0530-4305)"
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/date": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "2026-10-02"
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "\nThe **_Collections Ontology (CO)_** is an ontology for defining unordered collections (sets and bags) and ordered collections (or lists).\n\n![The main architecture of CO.](co.png)\n\nThe main class of CO is `co:Collection`, i.e. a group of objects that can be considered as a whole. \nEach collection is linked to the objects it contains by means of the property `co:element` (with `co:elementOf` as its inverse), and to the number of objects it contains by means of the property `co:size`. \nA collection has exactly one size.\n\nCO defines three kinds of collections:\n\n- `co:Set`, an unordered collection that cannot contain duplicate elements;\n- `co:Bag`, an unordered collection that can contain the same element more than once;\n- `co:List`, a bag in which the elements are ordered.\n\nSince the same object can be in a bag more than once, a bag is not only linked to its elements. \nEach occurrence of an object in a bag is a `co:Item`, and each list item in a list is a `co:ListItem`, which is a subclass of `co:Item`. \n\n![The representation of a set according to CO.](set.png)\n\nA `co:Set` is the simplest collection of CO. \nA set is directly linked to each of its members by means of `co:element`. \nNo item is necessary, since each object can be in a set only once. \nThe size of a set is the number of its elements.\n\n![The representation of a bag according to CO.](bag.png)\n\nA `co:Bag` is linked to its items by means of `co:item`. \nEach `co:Item` is one occurrence of an object in the bag, and it is linked to that object by means of the functional property `co:itemContent`. \nAn item cannot be the content of another item. \nTwo items of the same bag can have the same content: this is how a bag contains duplicates.\n\nThe property chain `co:item o co:itemContent` is a subproperty of `co:element`. \nThus, the elements of a bag are inferred from its items. \nThe size of a bag is the number of its items, not the number of its distinct elements.\n\n![The representation of a list according to CO.](list.png)\n\nA `co:List` is a bag in which all the items are `co:ListItem`s.\nA list has at most one first item (`co:firstItem`) and at most one last item (`co:lastItem`). \n\nEach list item has exactly one position in the list, starting from 1, specified by means of `co:index`. \nEach list item is linked to the list item that immediately follows it by means of `co:nextItem`, and to the list item that immediately precedes it by means of `co:previousItem`. \nThe property chain `co:item o co:nextItem` is a subproperty of `co:item`. Thus, it is sufficient to specify the first item of a list and the chain of `co:nextItem` relations: all the other items of the list are inferred.\n\nCO also includes some SWRL rules that complete the description of a list:\n\n- the index of a list item is the index of its previous item plus one;\n- two items of the same list with consecutive indexes are linked by `co:nextItem`;\n- the item of a list with index 1 is the first item of the list;\n- the size of a list is the index of its last item, and the item whose index is equal to the size of the list is the last item of the list;\n- a list item that is preceded or followed by itself raises an error (`error:hasError`).\n\n## Examples of use\n\nIn the following subsections, we introduce some examples to showcase how to use CO. \n\nThe prefixes that are used in all the examples provided below are defined as follows:\n\n        @prefix : <http://www.sparontologies.net/example/> .\n        @prefix co: <http://purl.org/co/> .\n        @prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .\n\n### A reading list as a set\n\nA reading list contains two papers. \nThe order of the papers is not relevant and each paper is in the reading list only once. \nThus, the reading list is a `co:Set` that is linked to its members with `co:element`.\n\n        :reading-list a co:Set ;\n            co:element :paper-1 , :paper-2 ;\n            co:size \"2\"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger .\n\n### In-text citations as a bag\n\nThe text of an article cites `:paper-1` twice and `:paper-2` once. \nThe order of the citations is not relevant, but the number of times each paper is cited is. \nThus, the citations are a `co:Bag` of three `co:Item`s: each item is one occurrence, and `co:itemContent` links the item to the cited paper. \nThe statements `:citations co:element :paper-1 , :paper-2` are inferred by means of the property chain `co:item o co:itemContent`.\n\n        :citations a co:Bag ;\n            co:item :citation-1 , \n                :citation-2 , \n                :citation-3 ;\n            co:size \"3\"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger .\n\n        :citation-1 a co:Item ; \n                co:itemContent :paper-1 .\n\n        :citation-2 a co:Item ; \n                co:itemContent :paper-2 .\n\n        :citation-3 a co:Item ; \n                co:itemContent :paper-1 .\n\n### The authors of a paper as a list\n\nA paper has three authors, and their order is relevant. \nThus, the authors are a `co:List` of three `co:ListItem`s. \nEach list item has a position (`co:index`), refers to one author (`co:itemContent`) and is linked to the list item that follows it (`co:nextItem`).\n\n        :authors a co:List ;\n            co:firstItem :author-1 ;\n            co:item :author-1 , :author-2 , :author-3 ;\n            co:lastItem :author-3 ;\n            co:size \"3\"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger .\n\n        :author-1 a co:ListItem ;\n            co:index \"1\"^^xsd:positiveInteger ;\n            co:itemContent :alice ;\n            co:nextItem :author-2 .\n\n        :author-2 a co:ListItem ;\n            co:index \"2\"^^xsd:positiveInteger ;\n            co:itemContent :bob ;\n            co:nextItem :author-3 .\n\n        :author-3 a co:ListItem ;\n            co:index \"3\"^^xsd:positiveInteger ;\n            co:itemContent :carol .\n\n## Competency Questions\n\nCO can be used for answering several questions related to different ways entities can be grouped into aggregates and organized into collections.\nIn the following subsections, some of them are introduced together with their respective SPARQL queries. \n\nThe prefixes that are used in all the SPARQL queries provided below are defined as follows:\n        \n        PREFIX : <http://www.sparontologies.net/example/>\n        PREFIX co: <http://purl.org/co/>\n\n### CQ1\n\nWhat are the entities that belong to a collection?\n\n        SELECT DISTINCT ?collection ?element\n        WHERE {\n            ?collection co:element|co:item/co:itemContent ?element .\n        }\n\n### CQ2\n\nHow many times does each entity appear in a bag?\n\n        SELECT ?element (COUNT(?item) AS ?occurrences)\n        WHERE {\n            :citations co:item ?item .\n            ?item co:itemContent ?element .\n        }\n        GROUP BY ?element\n\n### CQ3\n\nWhat is the size of a collection?\n\n        SELECT ?collection ?size\n        WHERE {\n            ?collection co:size ?size .\n        }\n\n### CQ4\n\nWhat are the first entity and the last entity of a list?\n\n        SELECT ?first ?last\n        WHERE {\n            :authors co:firstItem/co:itemContent ?first ;\n                co:lastItem/co:itemContent ?last .\n        }\n\n### CQ5\n\nWhich entity is at a given position of a list?\n\n        SELECT ?element\n        WHERE {\n            :authors co:item ?item .\n            ?item co:index ?index ;\n                co:itemContent ?element .\n            FILTER (?index = 2)\n        }\n\n    "
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/title": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Collections Ontology (CO)"
      }
    ],
